---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="10-Year Thesis (2026–2036)"
  description="Dinesh Pai's notes on where to invest in India over the next ten years: household income graduation, betting on what becomes scarce, and sovereign resilience in grid infrastructure, industrial machinery, and metals."
>
  <div class="thesis-page space-y-14">

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="border-b border-stone-800 dark:border-stone-200 pb-8">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mb-3">
        <span>Notes on the Next Ten Years</span>
        <time datetime="2026-10">2026 — 2036</time>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-serif font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 leading-[1.18] mb-5">
        Income graduation, consumer scarcity, and sovereign industrial resilience.
      </h1>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-base sm:text-[17px] text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          When I think about where to invest in India over the next ten years, I keep coming back to two simple themes.
        </p>
        <p>
          First, tens of millions of Indian families are moving up the income ladder into the middle and upper-middle brackets. When a household has disposable income left over after paying for rent and groceries, its spending shifts toward better housing, travel, entertainment, healthcare, and appliances. Because physical supply in India takes years to build, rising demand creates acute shortages. I want to back businesses solving what becomes scarce.
        </p>
        <p>
          Second, India still imports a surprising share of its core physical infrastructure—from electrical steel and high-voltage grid components to factory CNC machines and defense hardware. Building domestic resilience in these sectors is a priority that successive governments will continue to back over the next decade.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section Jump Links -->
      <nav aria-label="Thesis sections" class="mt-7 pt-4 border-t border-stone-300/80 dark:border-stone-800 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider">
        <a href="#income-slabs" class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400 hover:text-brand-light dark:hover:text-brand-dark no-underline">01 / Income Slabs</a>
        <a href="#tax-filings" class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400 hover:text-brand-light dark:hover:text-brand-dark no-underline">02 / Tax Filings</a>
        <a href="#what-gets-scarce" class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400 hover:text-brand-light dark:hover:text-brand-dark no-underline">03 / What Becomes Scarce</a>
        <a href="#sovereign-infra" class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400 hover:text-brand-light dark:hover:text-brand-dark no-underline">04 / Grid, Machinery & Metals</a>
        <a href="#patient-capital" class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400 hover:text-brand-light dark:hover:text-brand-dark no-underline">05 / Patient Capital</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <!-- 01 / INDIAN HOUSEHOLD INCOME SLABS & GRADUATION -->
    <section id="income-slabs" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-6">
      <div class="section-rule">
        <span>01 / Household Income Graduation</span>
        <span class="section-sub">From Pyramid to Diamond</span>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <p>
          To understand why consumer demand in India is changing, it helps to look at how Indian households are distributed across income slabs and how fast families are graduating upward.
        </p>
        <p>
          According to the ICE 360° household surveys by PRICE (<em>People Research on India's Consumer Economy</em>), India had roughly 301 million households in 2020–21. At the start of this decade, two-thirds of the population lived in the lowest two brackets—earning under ₹5 lakh a year per household. Over the ten years to 2030–31, the bottom two slabs shrink in absolute numbers while <strong>74 million additional households enter the middle class (₹5–30 lakh)</strong> and <strong>24 million additional households enter the affluent bracket (&gt;₹30 lakh)</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Table 1: Household Income Slabs -->
      <figure class="data-card">
        <div class="card-header">
          <div>
            <h2 class="card-title">Indian Households Across Annual Income Slabs (2020–21 vs. 2030–31E)</h2>
