      </figure>
    </section>

    <!-- 02 / INCOME TAX FILINGS TREND (2021 vs 2025/26) -->
    <section id="tax-filings" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-6">
      <div class="section-rule">
        <span>02 / What Income Tax Filings Show</span>
        <span class="section-sub">2021 vs. 2025–26 Slab Migration</span>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <p>
          Survey projections can sometimes feel abstract, so it is useful to check them against hard tax filings from the Central Board of Direct Taxes (CBDT) and the Income Tax e-Filing portal.
        </p>
        <p>
          Between Assessment Year 2021–22 (FY21) and FY 2025–26, the total number of individual tax returns grew by 29%, from <strong>6.37 crore to 8.22 crore</strong>. What matters is <em>where</em> that growth happened. Returns filed in the lowest slab (up to ₹5 lakh) actually fell by 20% as earners moved up and tax-rebate thresholds rose. Meanwhile, filers in the <strong>₹5–10 lakh slab rose 81%</strong>, filers earning <strong>₹10–50 lakh more than doubled from 72 lakh to 1.50 crore</strong>, and individuals declaring <strong>over ₹1 crore grew 128%</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Table 2: ITR Filings by Gross Total Income Slab -->
      <figure class="data-card">
        <div class="card-header">
          <div>
            <h2 class="card-title">Individual Income Tax Filings by Gross Income Slab (2021 vs. 2025–26)</h2>
            <p class="card-subtitle">Individual ITRs filed on the Income Tax Portal · Source: CBDT Time-Series & e-Filing Portal Statistics</p>
          </div>
          <span class="card-badge">6.37 Cr → 8.22 Cr</span>
        </div>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Gross Annual Income Slab</th>
                <th class="num">2021 Filers (AY22)</th>
                <th class="num">2025–26 Filers</th>
                <th class="num">Share of Filers (FY26)</th>
                <th class="num">5-Year Change</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Up to ₹5 Lakh</th>
                <td class="num">3.47 Cr</td>
                <td class="num">2.76 Cr</td>
                <td class="num">33.6%</td>
                <td class="num text-stone-500">-20%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">₹5 Lakh – ₹10 Lakh</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">2.12 Cr</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">3.84 Cr</td>
                <td class="num">46.7%</td>
                <td class="num pos">+81%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">₹10 Lakh – ₹50 Lakh</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">72.2 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">1.50 Cr</td>
                <td class="num">18.3%</td>
                <td class="num pos">+108%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">₹50 Lakh – ₹1 Crore</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">3.95 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">7.64 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num">0.93%</td>
                <td class="num pos">+93%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Above ₹1 Crore</th>
                <td class="num font-semibold">1.69 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">3.86 Lakh</td>
                <td class="num">0.47%</td>
                <td class="num pos">+128%</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
            <tfoot>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Total Individual Returns</th>
                <td class="num">6.37 Cr</td>
                <td class="num">8.22 Cr</td>
                <td class="num">100.0%</td>
                <td class="num">+29%</td>
              </tr>
            </tfoot>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="card-footnote">
          Two things stand out in this table. First, the number of Indians formally declaring over ₹10 lakh a year has more than doubled in five years to ~1.62 crore individuals. Second, ~1.62 crore people is still only ~1.1% of India's total population—which shows both how fast formal disposable income is compounding and how early we still are in this cycle.
        </p>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <!-- 03 / THEME 1: BETTING ON WHAT BECOMES SCARCE -->
    <section id="what-gets-scarce" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-6">
      <div class="section-rule">
        <span>03 / Theme 1: Consumer Scarcity</span>
        <span class="section-sub">Where Disposable Income Meets Tight Supply</span>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <p>
          When tens of millions of families cross ₹10 lakh in household income, basic necessities are already taken care of. Incremental income goes into upgrading how they live: better homes, holidays, dining and entertainment, preventive health, financial products, and household hardware.
        </p>
        <p>
          In software, if demand doubles, a company can spin up more cloud servers in an afternoon. In the physical world, when 50 million families simultaneously want better housing, a weekend holiday, clean dairy, or air conditioning, supply cannot adjust quickly. We saw this exact pattern play out in Japan in the 1970s, South Korea in the late 1980s, and China after 2008.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Table 3: Historical Asian Consumption Inflections (Compact columns, no multi-sentence bloat) -->
      <figure class="data-card">
        <div class="card-header">
          <div>
            <h2 class="card-title">Consumption Shifts After Crossing $3,000–$5,000 Per-Capita GDP</h2>
            <p class="card-subtitle">Historical comparison across East Asian economies and India's current trajectory</p>
          </div>
          <span class="card-badge">Asian Precedent</span>
        </div>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Country</th>
                <th>Decade</th>
                <th class="num">GDP / Capita Shift</th>
                <th>Fastest-Compounding Consumer Categories</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Japan</th>
                <td class="mono">1972 – 1980</td>
                <td class="num">$2,900 → $9,400</td>
                <td>Urban housing, domestic rail travel, home appliances (ACs, refrigerators)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">South Korea</th>
                <td class="mono">1987 – 1996</td>
                <td class="num">$3,500 → $13,000</td>
                <td>Apartment complexes, leisure & hospitality, consumer electronics, automobiles</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">China</th>
                <td class="mono">2008 – 2017</td>
                <td class="num">$3,400 → $8,800</td>
                <td>Domestic aviation & hotels, diagnostics, protein & dairy, cooling hardware</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">India</th>
                <td class="mono">2025 – 2035E</td>
                <td class="num font-semibold">$2,900 → $6,500+</td>
                <td>Quality housing, travel & leisure, clean food, wealth management, cooling & medtech</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <div class="space-y-4 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <p class="font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">
          Over the next decade, I am most interested in five areas where rising middle-class spending runs into genuine physical or trust bottlenecks:
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="thesis-list text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>
          <strong>Housing & Well-Run Communities:</strong> As nuclear families upgrade from basic apartments, the shortage is not in luxury skyscrapers—it is in thoughtfully designed, energy- and water-resilient communities with reliable execution. Developable urban land with clean titles and credible developers remains scarce.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Travel, Hospitality & Leisure Experiences:</strong> Airport capacity, Vande Bharat trains, and expressways have expanded much faster than good places to stay. Outside a handful of luxury chains, India has a massive shortage of clean, reliable mid-market hotels, homestays, and leisure destinations within a 3–4 hour drive of major cities.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Clean, Traceable Food & Nutrition:</strong> Higher incomes in India have unfortunately come alongside rising diabetes and metabolic disease. Families are actively looking for chemical-free staples, high-protein foods, and clean labels. The bottleneck here is not branding or quick-commerce delivery; it is farm-level sourcing, soil health, and cold-chain logistics (<a href="/portfolio" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-4">Akshayakalpa</a>, <a href="/portfolio" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-4">The Whole Truth</a>, <a href="/portfolio" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-4">Two Brothers</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Transparent Financial Services:</strong> As tens of millions of tax-filing earners move savings out of physical gold and bank deposits into mutual funds, equities, and insurance, they need unconflicted advice and software infrastructure to manage their money (<a href="/portfolio" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-4">Capitalmind</a>, <a href="/portfolio" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-4">Assetplus</a>, <a href="/portfolio" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-4">Mprofit</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scarce Consumer & Medical Hardware (Cooling & Diagnostics):</strong> Only ~9% of Indian households own an air conditioner today, compared to over 60% in China. As incomes rise in a warming climate, India will add over 100 million room ACs, commercial chillers, and agricultural cold-chains—creating strong demand for efficient BLDC motors, compressors, and thermal materials. On the healthcare side, as 150 million Indians cross age 60, we will need affordable point-of-care diagnostic devices and out-of-hospital rehabilitation centers across Tier-2 and Tier-3 towns.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- 04 / THEME 2: SOVEREIGN TECHNOLOGIES & INDUSTRIAL RESILIENCE -->
    <section id="sovereign-infra" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-6">
      <div class="section-rule">
        <span>04 / Theme 2: Sovereign Industrial Stack</span>
        <span class="section-sub">Grid Infrastructure, Machinery & Metals</span>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <p>
          My second theme is sovereign technologies and industrial resilience. Over the last few years, export controls and supply-chain disruptions have made one thing obvious: a country of 1.4 billion people cannot rely on imports for its power grid, factory machinery, critical metals, communications, and defense.
        </p>
        <p>
          Building domestic capability in these areas is not a short-term policy fad; it is something successive governments will have to support for the next twenty years. While space and defense get most of the headlines, I am equally interested in the industrial layers underneath them—<strong>grid infrastructure, industrial machinery, and specialty metals</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Table 4: Sovereign Industrial Bottlenecks (Compact columns) -->
      <figure class="data-card">
        <div class="card-header">
          <div>
            <h2 class="card-title">Key Physical Bottlenecks in India's Industrial Stack</h2>
            <p class="card-subtitle">Where import dependence or capacity shortages create long-term domestic opportunities</p>
          </div>
          <span class="card-badge">Sovereign Resilience</span>
        </div>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Industrial Layer</th>
                <th>Key Component / Material</th>
                <th class="num">Import Exposure / Lead Time</th>
                <th>Domestic Opportunity</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Grid Infrastructure</th>
                <td>HV Transformers & Switchgear</td>
                <td class="num">20–30 mo lead times</td>
                <td>Power transformers, BESS storage, solid-state protection, grid software</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="row-accent">
                <th scope="row">Critical Metals</th>
                <td>CRGO Steel & Rare-Earth Magnets</td>
                <td class="num">~85–90% imported</td>
                <td>Electrical steel, rare-earth-free motors (<a href="/portfolio" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-4">Naxatra</a>), copper & battery recycling</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Industrial Machinery</th>
                <td>Precision CNCs, Spindles & Test Rigs</td>
                <td class="num">70–80% imported</td>
                <td>Multi-axis CNCs, actuators, optical inspection rigs, factory robotics</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Sovereign Sensing</th>
                <td>SAR Satellites, Comms & Propulsion</td>
                <td class="num">Strategic export controls</td>
                <td>All-weather earth observation (<a href="/portfolio" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-4">Galaxeye</a>), solid propulsion (<a href="/portfolio" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-4">Spacefields</a>)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <ul class="thesis-list text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>
          <strong>Grid Infrastructure & Power Electronics:</strong> Generating solar power is no longer the bottleneck in India; evacuating and storing it is. As electric vehicles, air conditioners, factories, and data centers come online, our transmission and distribution grid needs massive upgrades. I am keen on companies building grid-scale battery storage, transformer components, thermal fire-safety materials (<a href="/portfolio" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-4">Voltseal</a>), and commercial clean-energy infrastructure (<a href="/portfolio" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-4">Neufin</a>, <a href="/portfolio" class="text-brand-light dark:text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-4">Solarsquare</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Industrial Machinery ("The Machines That Make the Machines"):</strong> Walk through almost any Indian factory assembling electronics, auto parts, or aerospace components today, and the high-precision CNC machines, spindles, and automated testing rigs are still imported from Germany, Japan, Taiwan, or China. As Indian manufacturing scales, localizing precision machine tools, industrial sensors, and factory automation is a massive multi-decade opportunity.
        </li>
        <li>
